From: be7000-openwrt <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: qca808x: pulse the QCA8084 SerDes system reset like stock does

The stock firmware's QCA8084 preinit enables SRDS0/1_SYS_CBCR and then
pulses their reset bit for 20 ms before anything touches the SerDes.
qca8084_pcs_set_mode() only de-asserts NSS_CC_SRDS1_SYS_ARES, which is
a no-op when the line was never asserted, so the SerDes block keeps
whatever state the chip reset left it in. Assert, wait 20 ms, de-assert.

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca8084_serdes.c b/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca8084_serdes.c
index 9cfbc29..cc279b8 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca8084_serdes.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca8084_serdes.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/clk-provider.h>
 #include <linux/dev_printk.h>
 #include <linux/mdio.h>
@@ -551,11 +552,20 @@ static int qca8084_pcs_set_mode(struct mdio_device *xpcs_mdiodev,
 	struct qca8084_xpcs_channel_priv xpcs_ch;
 	int ret, channel;
 
-	/* Enable clock and de-assert for PCS. */
+	/* Enable the PCS clock, then pulse its system reset. The stock
+	 * firmware resets the SerDes block this way (SRDS1_SYS_CBCR bit 2,
+	 * 20 ms) right after the clock is enabled; only de-asserting a reset
+	 * that was never asserted leaves the block as the hardware reset left
+	 * it.
+	 */
 	ret = clk_prepare_enable(pcs_data->clks[PCS_CLK]);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	ret = reset_control_assert(pcs_data->rstc);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	msleep(20);
 	ret = reset_control_deassert(pcs_data->rstc);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
